WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.141
I want to congratulate you on "Cobra Kai."

00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:06.250
I have watched I think almost all of them.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.510
I am loving the show,
I'm really enjoying it.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.890
It seems like the perfect show right now

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:18.560
because it's this nice dose of nostalgia.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.770
It's really fun and the show
has a great sense of humor,

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:25.100
so I'm enjoying it, it's
a great quarantine show.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.400
What's the response you're
hearing from people?

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.540
Well, I'll take the great
show no matter what,

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.250
but it is, I call it the
comfort food, you know,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.560
it's that big fat nostalgic embrace

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.250
and yet it's telling
relevant stories for today

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.050
and a great young cast

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.180
and it's just hitting on all cylinders

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.000
and that's this sort of blessing.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.050
I mean, this has been I
call it the second rebirth

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.250
of the rebirth of the original movie.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.570
You know, we came out a few
years ago on YouTube premium

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.340
and now on Netflix it's
just blown out of the water

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.440
all over the world and it's
a great time for the show

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.920
and it's nice to be an
alternative to the hell of it,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.190
where we are living with every day.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.160
I'll tell you how you're
affecting my life,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.130
which is one of the ways
you know, right now,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.320
in addition to quarantine,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.813
we also have so much smoke
in the air here in the west.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.880
You can't go outside.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.790
I have a treadmill in the basement.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:28.573
It's in a tiny room and
there is a small TV there

00:01:28.573 --> 00:01:32.376
and I will binge watch "Cobra
Kai" and I'm on the treadmill.

00:01:32.376 --> 00:01:35.197
And what happens is when it
builds up to a fight scene,

00:01:35.197 --> 00:01:38.250
I crank the treadmill

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.070
so I'm running at like 60
miles an hour, bare chested.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.310
It's not a pretty sight.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.620
I'll give you a picture
cause you clearly want one,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.990
but I'm running full out as the kid

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.210
is beating up the
bullies in the cafeteria.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.200
And I'm like, ahh!

00:01:54.274 --> 00:01:56.650
And my family comes running downstairs

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.320
because they think I've
had a major heart attack.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.696
A heart attack, right.

00:02:00.696 --> 00:02:03.090
(laughs)

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:08.090
And the show is laced with
this great 80's homage music.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.890
You know, at that time
the training montages

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.176
and all that stuff.

00:02:13.176 --> 00:02:16.400
It is kick ass in that
way and that's awesome.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.400
Well, I'm glad to help out in your cardio

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.233
and everything else.

00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:24.050
Yes, you mentioned 80's and nostalgia.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.260
I've got some pictures
here, I'm sure your life

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.470
has been being confronted
by these pictures.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.690
This is you, yeah exactly.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.220
First of all, let's sue
the guy who convinced you

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.053
to do that pose.

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:38.652
I know, I know, there's so
many, there's many others.

00:02:38.652 --> 00:02:40.235
I'm actually glad that was the one you,

00:02:40.235 --> 00:02:42.010
well, let's see what's next.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.970
Oh no no no, I've got this one too.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.733
Oh well that's from a
movie, that's from a movie.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.456
That's "Teachers," the
movie with Nick Nolte.

00:02:48.456 --> 00:02:51.500
I know, I know, I know, but
any kid who's wearing that hat

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.530
to school needs to know karate.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.096
That's right.

00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:56.490
(laughs)

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.827
You know it's funny,
because all these pictures--

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.413
You and I think are
just about the same age

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:09.413
the saving grace for me is
there are no photographs of me

00:03:09.517 --> 00:03:11.693
until I'm about 30, I was unphotographed.

00:03:11.693 --> 00:03:15.180
I'm like someone that
was born in the 1850s,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.260
there's no photograph of me

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.416
and then they start around
the time I'm 29 or 30.

00:03:19.416 --> 00:03:24.230
What's it like for your kids
when they're on the internet

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:28.776
and this pops up and they
say, "Dad, what the (beep)?"

00:03:28.776 --> 00:03:31.310
Yeah, what the (beep), and
it gives them leverage,

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.863
great amounts of leverage.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.450
It's you know, I mean,
what are you going to do?

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.710
It's kinda fun cause when my kids were--

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.217
My daughter was like
four when she first saw

00:03:43.217 --> 00:03:44.823
"The Karate Kid," maybe five.

00:03:46.115 --> 00:03:48.157
And so it was one of those things,

00:03:48.157 --> 00:03:52.115
I was doing a Broadway
musical around the country

00:03:52.115 --> 00:03:55.070
called "How to Succeed" and I
was playing lead in this show.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.172
And every city we would go
to these Planet Hollywood's,

00:03:58.172 --> 00:04:00.110
which were big deals
back in the early 90s,

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:03.820
and we'd grab lunch there with my kids

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.750
and my daughter would
see this memorabilia up

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.387
and ask these questions like,

00:04:07.387 --> 00:04:09.490
"Why are the people standing on one leg

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.760
and holding their arms up in
the air when they see daddy

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.540
and what is that headband,
why are they wearing it?"

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.010
And then finally it was like well,

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.560
you know, it's time to
sit you down, honey.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.640
Daddy's not like all the other daddies.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.970
And then I showed her the
film at four years old.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.560
You showed her "Karate Kid" at four?

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.460
Yeah, five years old, like it was five

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.570
because we got back when she
was five and I showed her that

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.437
and thinking that I would make
her proud and not thinking

00:04:34.437 --> 00:04:37.516
that I get my ass kicked
in every other scene.

00:04:37.516 --> 00:04:42.240
And she's watching her dad be
beaten to a pulp constantly

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.910
running out of the room.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.495
So that was an epic parent fail by me.

00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:50.055
Yeah, you scarred your child for life.

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:53.290
Although, I have to say
my children at any point

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.840
in their lives would love
nothing more than to see

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.900
footage of me being beaten.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.260
Yes, it might've changed to that

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.860
when she got through middle
school, but at five she really,

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.253
you know, I was a hero for a moment.

00:05:05.253 --> 00:05:09.690
And my son on the flip side
found all those teen magazine

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.332
kind of pictures when he
was about the same age

00:05:12.332 --> 00:05:17.332
and ran into the family room
to my daughter and said,

00:05:17.415 --> 00:05:22.375
"Look, dad was huge and we missed it."

00:05:22.375 --> 00:05:24.458
(laughs)

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.991
So fortunately for me right
now with the "Cobra Kai" show,

00:05:29.991 --> 00:05:33.930
Dad may not be that huge,
but they're not missing it.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:37.410
Well, you guys have been
like the number one show on--

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.220
Yeah we are huge, let's say it, I'm huge.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.891
I mean, let's just say that.

00:05:40.891 --> 00:05:44.970
And you know, another thing I
wanted to say about the show

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.012
that I really love is
there's a real funny sense

00:05:48.012 --> 00:05:51.720
of humor and self
awareness about the show.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.720
So I love, there are the
scenes where Johnny Lawrence,

00:05:57.194 --> 00:06:01.410
you know, the bully in
the original "Karate Kid,"

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.780
he's struggling and he doesn't understand

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.650
that the 80s are over and watching scenes

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.560
where he's like trying to hock his,

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.297
he's trying to sell his Atari console

00:06:11.297 --> 00:06:13.797
and we don't give a
shit about this anymore.

00:06:13.797 --> 00:06:16.700
He's very good at tapping into,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.316
it's almost in a comical
way, he doesn't understand

00:06:20.316 --> 00:06:23.300
that it hasn't been the
80s for a long time.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.910
He missed, someone didn't tell
him that things have changed.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.116
Yeah, it's refreshing to
have a character like that

00:06:29.116 --> 00:06:32.870
who is not politically correct
in any way, shape or form

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:37.020
and represent a lot of
what many people think,

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.500
but can't, or won't say, so
I think there's a release

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.839
with that because we're so
overprotective of everything.

00:06:42.839 --> 00:06:47.760
The antihero and you know,
you root for that redemption.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.736
That's the cool thing about
the show is it really,

00:06:50.736 --> 00:06:52.970
your allegiance to each character

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.120
can change episode to
episode cause it dives more

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.970
into the gray areas of who these guys are.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.270
And even the young characters,

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.760
as opposed to the black and white

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.790
of what "Karate Kid" was
good or evil, you know.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.440
Well, what's happened interestingly enough

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.660
is that initially, "Karate
Kid" and I remembered seeing it

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.334
in the theater when it came
out was about, you know,

00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:19.334
you are clearly the protagonist,
you're the underdog,

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.570
you're the hero and then it's clear

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.030
who the bully is, Johnny Lawrence.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.200
What happened over time,
and it's kind of a tribute

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.359
to the film is that people got
into these discussion groups

00:07:30.359 --> 00:07:34.230
and it sort of became part
of almost a cultural question

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.210
of who is the real bully?

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.036
Because he has the cruel
sensei and you have

00:07:40.036 --> 00:07:44.716
the nice sensei, you take
his girlfriend, you know,

00:07:44.716 --> 00:07:49.580
those are the intellectual
discussions of our time,

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.997
basically.

00:07:50.997 --> 00:07:52.500
(laughs)

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.423
You've laid it out beautifully.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.210
It has become pop culture.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.500
I mean, it's really become that.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.140
There are fan videos, theories,
the crane kick was illegal.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:05.370
You know, you say he took his girlfriend.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:06.823
Well, you know, she was,

00:08:06.823 --> 00:08:10.060
I mean we could go on and on about that.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:14.321
I defend LaRusso, but
it's fun that people care.

00:08:14.321 --> 00:08:18.279
I mean, they've cared for 35, 36 years

00:08:18.279 --> 00:08:22.283
and now it's lifted to another level.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.910
But Ralph, Ralph be honest.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:27.020
There were times when you wake
up in the middle of the night

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:31.400
covered in sweat screaming,
the crane kick was legal.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.881
It's totally legal.
More often than you know.

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:37.100
(laughs)

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.230
I must confess on the Conan O'Brien show.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:41.940
Do you ever do that,
you're getting a latte

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.810
and someone's just giving you a look.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:45.833
It was legal, it was legal!

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.801
I'm doing that the whole week now.

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:53.770
I love it.
Every day this week

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:54.603
I'm doing that.

